WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.187 --> 00:00:01.020
DUSTIN: Hey, Martin.

00:00:01.020 --> 00:00:02.650
What're you doing?

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:03.910
MARTIN: Oh, hi, Dustin.

00:00:03.910 --> 00:00:07.090
I like reading articles about
serverless on Hacker News,

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:10.300
but I don't have time to
check the website every day.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.160
I found an API to give
me the latest articles,

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.610
and I've heard about
scheduled Cron Jobs,

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.027
but I don't know
how to combine them.

00:00:18.027 --> 00:00:20.110
DUSTIN: Hmm, you know that
sounds like it might be

00:00:20.110 --> 00:00:23.470
a good job for Cloud Scheduler.

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:27.040
MARTIN: And we can use that
with Python in the cloud?

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:27.680
DUSTIN: Yep.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:28.555
Let me tell you more.

00:00:28.555 --> 00:00:31.739
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:38.430 --> 00:00:40.530
Python is often
called a glue language

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:43.050
because it's a powerful
tool for easily combining

00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:44.970
multiple different systems.

00:00:44.970 --> 00:00:47.820
Part of this is because Python
has great client library

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.300
support, which lets you interact
with lots of APIs, products,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:53.790
and services from the
comfort of Python.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.710
But it doesn't just
end with the cloud.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.260
Python is useful for
combining just about anything

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.740
with anything else.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:02.610
So what are you trying
to glue together, Martin?

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:05.010
MARTIN: Well, I found this
Hacker News API, which

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.620
gives me the current top posts.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.480
I wanted it to email
me whenever there's

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.000
a new post about
serverless, so I never

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.750
miss an interesting article.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:17.250
DUSTIN: I think we could easily
do this with Cloud Scheduler

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.450
and Python.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.700
The first step would be
writing a Python script, which

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.760
can get the top stories
from the Hacker News API,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.040
then iterate over every story.

00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:28.140
If any of their titles
match serverless,

00:01:28.140 --> 00:01:30.460
it'll send you an
email with the links.

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:33.540
We'll use the request library
to make an HTTP request

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.880
to the URL for the
API, which happens

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.910
to be hosted on Firebase,
a Google Cloud product.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:42.180
We'll get all the top stories,
iterating over each one.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.010
Then if any of
the stories match,

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:45.870
we'll use some
custom code to send

00:01:45.870 --> 00:01:48.780
an email formatted with all
of our serverless stories.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.600
We can put this all in a
single Python function.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.180
We can then deploy this
app to Cloud Functions.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.630
After deploying, we get a
public URL for our function.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:58.590
MARTIN: Cool.

00:01:58.590 --> 00:02:00.660
So whenever I visit
this URL it will

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:04.080
email me any new articles
on the front page?

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:04.660
DUSTIN: Yep.

00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:07.030
And the last step is
running it on a schedule.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:10.120
For this, we use Cloud Scheduler
to create a new job, which

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.780
runs once a day.

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.860
Cron jobs are scheduled at
recurring intervals specified

00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:16.837
using the Unix cron format.

00:02:16.837 --> 00:02:18.670
You can define the
schedule so that your job

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:20.890
runs multiple times
a day, or runs

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.050
on specific days and months.

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:26.950
We'll schedule our job to
run once a day at midnight.

00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:29.530
We'll use the gcloud
scheduler jobs create command

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:32.680
to create a job which calls
our function on the schedule.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.650
We can use gcloud scheduler
jobs list to confirm

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:37.300
that our job was created.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:39.580
And we can use
gcloud scheduler jobs

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:43.000
run to run it once
regardless of the schedule.

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.800
MARTIN: Oh, I got the email.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:45.760
This is great.

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:47.680
I'll get any serverless
articles delivered

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.810
right to my inbox
every day without me

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:51.200
having to do a thing.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.750
Thanks.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:53.630
DUSTIN: Of course.

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:55.980
Anything else I
can help you with?

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.380
MARTIN: Actually, Dustin,
there is one thing.

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:00.090
I'm often late to meetings.

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:03.890
Do you think Cloud Scheduler
in Python can fix that too?

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:04.390
DUSTIN: Hmm.

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:07.013
I don't think I
can help you there.

00:03:07.013 --> 00:03:07.680
MARTIN: Oh well.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:11.030
[MUSIC PLAYING]

